    "lawCode": "Amd §10, Gen Muni L; amd §§454, 237, 234, 383, 86 & 87, add §§454-a & 454-b, Bank L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S1453\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general municipal law and the banking law, in\nrelation to allowing credit unions, savings banks, savings and loan\nassociations and federal savings associations to accept and secure\ndeposits from municipal corporations; to direct the department of finan-\ncial services to conduct a study and issue a report concerning the\nimpact of allowing certain financial institutions to accept local\ngovernment public deposits; and providing for the repeal of certain\nprovisions upon the expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo allow savings banks, savings and loan associations and federal\nsavings banks to accept deposits from municipalities and allow credit\nunions to accept deposits under certain limitations.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends section 10 of the general municipal law\nas it relates to changing the definition of \"bank\" to include savings\nbanks and savings and loan associations.\n\nSection two of the bill amends section 10 of the general municipal law\nas it relates to allowing credit unions to accept deposits from a local\ngovernment with certain limitations.\n\nSection three of the bill amends the banking law as it relates to allow-\ning credit unions to accept deposits from local governments if a branch\nor principal office is within a credit union's jurisdiction.\n\nSection four of the bill amends the banking law as it relates to requir-\ning credit unions to pledge assets or furnish other security for depos-\nits accepted from local governments.\n\nSection five of the bill amends the banking law as it relates to requir-\ning community investment by credit unions that accept municipal depos-\nits.\n\nSection six of the bill amends the banking law as it relates to removing\nrestrictions on savings banks from accepting deposits from a local\ngovernment.\n\nSection seven of the bill amends the banking law as it relates to\nrequiring savings banks to pledge assets or furnish other security for\ndeposits accepted from local governments.\n\nSection eight of the bill amends the banking law as it relates to\nrequiring savings and loan associations to pledge assets or furnish\nother security for deposits accepted from local governments.\n\nSection nine of the bill relates to requiring a study by the department\nof financial services on the impact of certain financial institutions\naccepting municipal deposits.\n\n\nSection ten of the bill amends the banking law as it relates to changing\nthe definition of a community bank institution for purposes of the\ncommunity bank deposit program.\n\nSection eleven of the bill amends the banking law as it relates to\nincrease the amount of deposits allowable under the community bank\ndeposit program and requires an annual report on the program.\n\nSection eleven of the bill relates to deposits held in credit unions at\nexpiration of the act.\n\nSection twelve of the bill relates to the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe current law restricting municipal deposits to commercial banks is an\nanachronism, stemming from the days when commercial banks were the most\nsecure option for these types of investments.\n\nAt that time, savings banks, savings and loan associations and credit\nunions, were smaller, less secure entities that primarily served indi-\nvidual investors by providing investment opportunities and offering\nloans, particularly mortgages to residents of the communities they\nserved. However, savings banks, savings and loan associations and credit\nunions are now on par with commercial banks.  In many of the services\nthey provide as well as the security they offer to their investors.\nAllowing savings banks, savings and loans and credit unions to accept\nmunicipal deposits would be beneficial to the communities they are serv-\ning by offering a competitive alternative to commercial banks, which are\noften large corporate entities with no real affiliation or commitment to\nthe community they serve.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022 PASSED SENATE\n\n2022 referred to banks\n\n2021 REPORTED AND COMMITTED TO SENATE FINANCE\n\n2021 referred to assembly banks\n\n2020 REFERRED TO SENATE BANKS\n\n2020 referred to assembly ways and means\n\n2019 REFERRED TO SENATE BANKS\n\n2019 referred to assembly banks\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone to the state\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law; provided that section two, three, four, five and nine of\nthe act shall expire and be deemed repealed December 31, 2025.\n",
